</div></div><div class="sqs-block website-component-block sqs-block-website-component sqs-block-html html-block" data-block-css="[&quot;https://definitions.sqspcdn.com/website-component-definition/static-assets/website.components.html/7625cf5e-824a-409c-844c-dc15103c89a9_941/website.components.html.styles.css&quot;]" data-block-scripts="[&quot;https://definitions.sqspcdn.com/website-component-definition/static-assets/website.components.html/7625cf5e-824a-409c-844c-dc15103c89a9_941/website.components.html.visitor.js&quot;]" data-block-type="1337" data-definition-name="website.components.html" data-sqsp-block="text" data-website-component-id="yui_3_17_2_1_1740460242971_21217" id="block-yui_3_17_2_1_1740460242971_21217"><div class="sqs-block-content"><div class="sqs-text-block-container">
  
  <div class="sqs-html-content" data-sqsp-text-block-content><p class="" style="white-space:pre-wrap;">NASA and Rocket Lab initially viewed a beach landing as the sole path for rocket delivery, since other infrastructure like a new bridge would require 1-2 years of additional analysis under the National Environmental Policy Act (NEPA) before construction could even begin.</p><p class="" style="white-space:pre-wrap;">However, timelines only deteriorated from there. Whereas in July, a September 2024 - March 2025 window was proposed, by October 2024, these plans had changed. An October 15 document submission to NOAA for compliance with Essential Fish Habitat (EFH) assessments showed that Rocket Lab and NASA had opened a new window for landings, now moved back an entire year and starting in September 2025:</p></div>


  
